" tabindex="0"><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-button docs-ml-promotion-no-button">NO, THANKS</span></span></span><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-container"><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-button-wrapper docs-ml-promotion-yes-button-wrapper" role="button" tabindex="0"><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-button docs-ml-promotion-yes-button">USE THE APP</span></span></span></div></div></div></div><div id="docs-ml-header-id" class="docs-ml-header"><span class="docs-ml-header-item docs-ml-header-drive-link"><div id="docs-ml-header-drive-link-aria-label" style="display:none;">Go to Drive</div><a href="https://drive.google.com/u/2?usp=docs_web" aria-labelledby="docs-ml-header-drive-link-aria-label" role="button"><div class="docs-ml-header-icon-container"><div class="docs-ml-icon docs-ml-header-icon docs-ml-arrow-back-dir"><div class="docs-ml-img-container docs-ml-img docs-ml-arrow-back-icon"></div></div></div></a></span><span class="docs-ml-header-item docs-ml-header-document-title"><div class="docs-ml-header-document-title-text">Hermes MCP Integration Architecture</div></span><div id="docs-ml-header-edit-button-aria-label" style="display:none;">Edit</div><span class="docs-ml-header-item docs-ml-header-edit-button" aria-labelledby="docs-ml-header-edit-button-aria-label" role="button" tabindex="0"><div class="docs-ml-header-icon-container"><div class="docs-ml-icon docs-ml-header-icon"><div class="docs-ml-img-container docs-ml-img docs-ml-edit-icon"></div></div></div></span></div><div class="app-container"><div class="doc-container"><div class="doc"><div class="doc-content" style="background-color:#ffffff;max-width:468pt;padding:72pt 72pt 72pt 72pt"><h1 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:24pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:24pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes Agent Integration Surface and External Capability Architecture</span></h1><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes Agent incorporates external capabilities without core engine modification through four primary integration mechanisms: out-of-process Model Context Protocol (MCP) clients, an in-process Python plugin framework, managed gateway connectors, and sandboxed execution runtimes. Procedural knowledge is decoupled from runtime execution: Skills provide on-demand contextual instructions loaded directly into the model prompt, whereas tools, MCP servers, and plugins supply the executable functions.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">When native Hermes capabilities are insufficient, the architecture directs capability expansion outward. First, in-process Python plugins extend core abstraction classes (such as web search, memory providers, and context engines) or attach lifecycle hooks. Second, external services and standard developer tools connect over local subprocesses (stdio) or remote network transports (Streamable HTTP and SSE) using the MCP client. Third, remote software-as-a-service (SaaS) ecosystems link through Nous Portal OAuth connectors. Finally, unstructured tasks and command-line interfaces execute through terminal backends or subagent task delegation.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The system boundary mandates that high-throughput data pipelines, heavy media transformations, and long-running stateful workflows reside outside the agent context loop. Hermes acts as an analytical director: it generates structured commands, coordinates execution parameters, and inspects lightweight metadata or file paths, while specialized external worker processes execute compute-intensive workloads asynchronously.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Executive Architectural Summary</span></h2><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">External capability acquisition in Hermes Agent is governed by explicit structural boundaries across the runtime stack, ensuring that capabilities can be extended cleanly without modifying core repository code.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">External Capability Acquisition Paths</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes acquires capabilities through four discrete integration doors:</span></p><ol class="lst-kix_list_1-0 start" start="1" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The built-in Model Context Protocol client connects to external tool servers over local process pipes or remote HTTP streams.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The native Python plugin system mounts custom tools, lifecycle hooks, CLI commands, and specialized provider implementations in-process.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Managed portal connectors provide access to remote third-party SaaS applications via Tool Search.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The terminal and code-execution engines allow the model to interact directly with local binaries, CLI tools, scripts, and containerized runtimes.</span></li></ol><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The MCP Integration Model</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The MCP client is implemented natively within tools/mcp_tool.py (~1050 lines of Python) and supports both local stdio subprocesses and remote Streamable HTTP/SSE endpoints. Configuration is managed via the mcp_servers dictionary in ~/.hermes/config.yaml or interactively through the hermes mcp CLI subcommand. External tools are automatically discovered via JSON-RPC methods (tools/list, resources/list, prompts/list), namespaced with double underscores (mcp__&lt;server&gt;__&lt;tool&gt;), and passed into the LLM context or indexed into the dynamic Tool Search catalog. Hermes supports advanced MCP primitives, including server-initiated LLM inference (sampling/createMessage), interactive form prompts (elicitation/create), and reverse-mode stdio hosting (hermes mcp serve).</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The Skill to Tool to MCP Operational Triad</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Skills and tools occupy fundamentally distinct operational planes. A Skill is a Markdown-based procedural workflow instruction conforming to the agentskills.io standard; it contains no compiled execution logic. Tools (whether native, plugin-based, or MCP-driven) provide executable handlers that expose JSON Schema definitions to the model. Skills instruct the model on when, why, and in what order to invoke tools. Once an MCP server connects, its tools register alongside native tools in the execution dispatch table, allowing Skills to reference and orchestrate MCP tools with execution parity.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Existence of a Distinct Plugin Architecture</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes operates a native Python plugin architecture managed by PluginManager, which is architecturally separate from MCP. While MCP provides process-isolated, language-agnostic tool integration over JSON-RPC, the Hermes plugin system allows in-process Python code to attach to agent lifecycle events (pre_tool_call, post_tool_call), inject slash commands, define top-level CLI commands, and implement core Abstract Base Classes (ABCs) such as WebSearchProvider, MemoryProvider, ContextEngine, and TerminalEnvironmentProvider.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Out-of-Core Extensibility Scope</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Operators can add extensive capabilities without altering the Hermes core repository:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_2-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Any local or remote MCP server can be mounted via configuration.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Custom Python plugins can be placed in ~/.hermes/plugins/ or packaged as pip entry points.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Specialized provider backends for LLM inference, web search, memory, context compression, and terminal sandboxing can be dropped into plugin subdirectories.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Procedural skills can be created in ~/.hermes/skills/.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Any system binary, script, or CLI utility can be executed through the terminal engine.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Systems Outside the Extension Boundary</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes does not support in-band binary streaming across tool boundaries; large media assets, raw video frames, and heavy data streams cannot pass through tool payloads and must be referenced via the filesystem. Furthermore, long-running stateful workflows, non-deterministic video render engines, and high-throughput data processing pipelines must remain decoupled from the agent loop, delegated instead to specialized external workers. Mid-session structural changes to tool schemas are also restricted, as dynamic schema manipulation during an active conversation breaks prompt-cache continuity.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes Native Tool Architecture and Runtime Execution</span></h2><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes structures tools as modular functions defined by a JSON Schema, an execution handler, a prerequisite environment check, and an assigned logical toolset. The native tool runtime operates through a centralized in-memory registry (tools.registry.registry), which exposes static tools and dynamically mounts external extensions.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Tool Abstraction, Metadata, and Schema Representation</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Native tools are defined declaratively. Each tool module specifies a JSON Schema following the JSON Schema draft-07 specification, establishing parameter types, properties, descriptions, and required keys. The registry enforces a strict execution contract:</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Handlers receive an arguments dictionary parsed from model outputs along with session metadata keyword arguments (such as task_id). Handlers must never raise unhandled exceptions; all operational failures, network exceptions, or validation errors must be trapped internally and returned as a JSON string matching the format {"error": "message"}. Handlers must serialize successful execution output using json.dumps() rather than returning raw Python dictionaries or binary primitives. A boolean check function (check_fn) executes during startup toolset assembly; if required environment variables or binary dependencies are missing, the tool is omitted from the session schema without raising fatal initialization errors.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Courier&quot;;font-style:normal">{<br> &nbsp;[span_256](start_span)[span_256](end_span)"name": "weather",<br> &nbsp;"description": "Get current weather for a location.",<br> &nbsp;"parameters": {<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;"type": "object",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;"properties": {<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"location": {<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"type": "string",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"description": "City name or coordinates"<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;},<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"units": {<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"type": "string",<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"enum": ["metric", "imperial"],<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;"default": "metric"<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;}<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;},<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;"required": ["location"]<br> &nbsp;}<br>}<br></span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Toolsets and Namespace Segmentation</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Tools are grouped into named toolsets (e.g., file, terminal, web, browser, memory, code_execution, delegation). Toolsets can be defined as core groupings, composite aliases (e.g., debugging, which merges file, terminal, and web), or deployment-specific platform configurations (e.g., hermes-cli, hermes-telegram). When an MCP server is configured, Hermes dynamically generates an isolated toolset slug prefixed with mcp-&lt;server_name&gt;, allowing administrators to include or exclude entire external servers through platform configuration profiles.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Progressive Disclosure via Tool Search</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">When extensive MCP server suites or third-party plugins are mounted simultaneously, JSON Schema representations can consume tens of thousands of tokens, exhausting model context windows and degrading prompt cache efficiency. Hermes resolves this via Tool Search, an opt-in progressive disclosure layer.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Under Tool Search, non-core tools (all MCP tools, non-core plugin tools, and remote connectors) are stripped from the active model tools array. The core capability set (_HERMES_CORE_TOOLS, containing terminal, read_file, write_file, patch, search_files, todo, memory, web_search, web_extract, clarify, execute_code, and delegate_task) remains statically loaded. The deferred catalog is mediated through three deterministic bridge tools:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_3-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">tool_search(queries, limit) executes a BM25 ranking pass over the names and descriptions of deferred tools, returning matched tool names and parameter overviews.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">tool_describe(names) retrieves the complete, unabridged JSON Schema for one or more requested tools, appending them into the conversation context.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">tool_call(calls) accepts an array of name-argument objects, validates parameters against the deferred schemas locally, and dispatches execution to the corresponding MCP server or plugin handler.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Tool Execution Sequence</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The operational lifecycle of tool invocation within Hermes proceeds through a deterministic, six-phase sequence:</span></p><ol class="lst-kix_list_4-0 start" start="1" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Context Construction and Filtering: At session initialization, the runtime scans configured toolsets, executes check_fn verifications, filters out excluded tools, and renders the active JSON Schema list (or mounts the Tool Search bridge tools) into the model's system prompt payload.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Model Selection: The LLM produces a structured function call containing the target tool name and a JSON-encoded parameter payload.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Dispatch Routing and Policy Verification: The parent runtime intercepts the call. If the tool belongs to the Tool Search bridge, arguments undergo local schema coercion before routing. If approvals are enabled (appro[span_280](start_span)[span_280](end_span)vals.mode set to manual), destructive actions or shell patterns matching deny rules trigger interactive CLI prompts or messaging gateway cards.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Handler Execution: The registered handler invokes the underlying code. For native tools, this is an in-process Python callable. For plugins, execution flows through in-process callbacks bounded by a 30-second timeout. For MCP servers, the client serializes a JSON-RPC request across stdio or HTTP/SSE. For programmatic scripts (execute_code), execution runs in a child process communicating back to the host via a Unix domain socket RPC.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Payload Normalization and Sanitization: Raw output is validated. Terminal exits map process signals to diagnostic descriptions. MCP outputs are inspected: invisible Unicode TAG smuggling vectors (U+E0000â€“U+E007F) are stripped, and protocol-reserved keys are pruned while preserving vendor _meta structures.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Observation Injection: The final JSON string is wrapped into a tool response turn and appended to session history, triggering the subsequent model reasoning step.</span></li></ol><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Model Context Protocol Client Implementation</span></h2><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes contains a built-in MCP client implemented in tools/mcp_tool.py, providing native Model Context Protocol integration without requiring external bridge daemons.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Transports, Protocol Standards, and Network Topologies</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes supports the official MCP specification across two primary transport mechanisms:</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">For local subprocess execution (stdio), Hermes spawns local child processes via command, args, and env parameters, exchanging newline-delimited JSON-RPC messages over standard input and standard output. Subprocess environments are scrubbed of parent API keys and system secrets.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">For remote endpoints (Streamable HTTP and SSE), Hermes connects directly to remote HTTP servers via network requests. It defaults to the Streamable HTTP protocol but supports Server-Sent Events when configured with transport: "sse". Network connections adhere to local and environment proxy rules (HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY, ALL_PROXY, and NO_PROXY), automatically normalizing SOCKS schemes.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Network security for remote HTTP servers includes TLS certificate verification, custom CA bundle assignment (ssl_verify: "/path/to/ca.pem"), and mutual TLS (mTLS) client certificate authentication (client_cert configured as a single PEM path, a [cert, key] pair, or an encrypted [cert, key, password] 3-tuple).</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Authentication and Credential Resolution</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Authentication across MCP servers is handled through three primary mechanisms:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_5-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Static Tokens and Headers: Configured directly in mcp_servers.&lt;name&gt;.headers (e.g., Authorization: "Bearer ${API_KEY}"), resolving secrets from the environment or ~/.hermes/.env.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">OAuth 2.1 with PKCE: Configured via auth: oauth on HTTP servers. Hermes initiates the OAuth 2.1 Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE) flow, performing metadata discovery, spawning a local browser for authorization, exchanging tokens, and caching credentials under ~/.hermes/mcp-tokens/&lt;server&gt;.json (or profile-scoped subdirectories). Refresh tokens are handled automatically. Under a multi-tenant gateway, OAuth connections are isolated per profile and never shared across users.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Pre-Registered OAuth without Dynamic Client Registration (DCR): When remote servers lack DCR support, Hermes prompts the operator for client_id and client_secret during hermes mcp add, binding a local callback loopback (e.g., http://localhost:27890/callback) to finalize authorization.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Server Registration and Configuration Specification</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">MCP servers are configured in the mcp_servers block within ~/.hermes/config.yaml. Configuration keys support variable expansion using standard ${VAR} and Cursor-compatible ${env:VAR} syntax, alongside filesystem variables such as ${userHome} and ${workspaceFolder}.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Courier&quot;;font-style:normal">mcp_servers:<br> &nbsp;github:<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;command: "npx"<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;args: ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;env:<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN: "${GITHUB_TOKEN}"<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;enabled: true<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;timeout: 300<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;connect_timeout: 60<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;tools:<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;include:<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;- "list_is[span_113](start_span)[span_113](end_span)sues"