t:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Any character within the server name or tool identifier that violates alphanumeric naming standards (such as hyphens, spaces, or dots) is automatically sanitized to an underscore (_) during registration. However, tools.include and tools.exclude configuration blocks require the server's original raw naming strings.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Server-Initiated Protocols: Sampling and Elicitation</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes supports bi-directional MCP primitives, permitting external servers to invoke agent intelligence or solicit user input:</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Sampling (sampling/createMessage) is enabled by default, allowing an MCP server to request LLM inference from the host agent. Hermes bounds this capability using sliding-window rate limiters (max_rpm: 10), maximum token ceilings (max_tokens_cap: 4096), per-request timeouts (30s), tool-loop depth bounds (max_tool_rounds: 5), and explicit model allowlists (allowed_models).</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Elicitation (elicitation/create) is enabled by default, permitting an MCP tool to interrupt mid-execution to request structured user input. Form-mode prompts route directly to CLI prompts or gateway chat UI buttons, governed by a 300-second timeout. URL-mode elicitation is rejected outright as an unsupported security risk.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Bi-Directional Hosting: Hermes as an MCP Server</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes can reverse roles and operate as an out-of-process stdio MCP server via hermes mcp serve. This allows IDEs and external orchestrators (such as Claude Code, Cursor, or Zed) to consume Hermes' multi-platform messaging fabric. When acting as a server, Hermes exposes 10 messaging and approval tools (conv[span_114](start_span)[span_114](end_span)ersations_list, conversation_get, messages_read, attachments_fetch, events_poll, events_wait, messages_send, channels_list, permissions_list_open, and permissions_respond), reading session state from ~/.hermes/state.db and bridging real-time events over a background polling thread.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0